4. W1 - LINKS <-> PUBLIC PAGE SYNC
==============================
GOAL: whatever the owner does to the items on their page - add, edit, delete, reorder, enable/disable - is saved to Firebase immediately and shows on the public page within seconds, for EVERY item type, with no ghost items (deleted but still visible) and no missing items (added but not visible).

ITEM TYPES: enumerate them from the code registry behind the "Add to your page" sheet (Social, Players, Links, Support & shop, Forms & utilities, Layout): plain links, social cards (all platforms), players (YouTube, Twitch, Spotify, SoundCloud, ...), forms, support & shop items, section headers, and social icons (header/footer social bar). Test every type with every operation, including items created through the "Paste a link" box.

W1.1 TRACE. For each operation x item type, trace: UI event -> client handler/state -> network request (URL, method, status, payload) -> server route/action -> Clerk user id present and ownership check -> database write (awaited? what did it return?) -> the document(s)/fields the public page reads -> render -> cache layer. Add TEMPORARY structured logging (never secrets or personal data) and write down the FIRST hop where the dashboard and the public page diverge. For DELETE specifically, determine which of these happens today: (a) the document is removed; (b) it is removed only from the dashboard list but not from the stored order list/array, section reference or a denormalized public snapshot; (c) it is soft-deleted with a flag the public page ignores; (d) the request fails silently; (e) it is "resurrected" by a later stale write (autosave or optimistic state re-saving the old list); (f) it is removed in the database but the public page is served from a stale cache.

W1.2 CHECK THESE CAUSES, MOST LIKELY FIRST (confirm with evidence, then apply the smallest fix):
a. Public page cache never invalidated. After EVERY successful add/edit/delete/reorder/toggle, trigger on-demand revalidation (path and/or tag per profile) for the canonical lowercase handle - awaited, not fire-and-forget - and keep a short TTL (60 s or less) as a safety net. Make sure CDN/edge caches honor it (consistent cache headers: s-maxage + stale-while-revalidate; tag/path purge where the platform supports it).
b. Whole-list overwrite. The dashboard writes the complete items list on every change from a possibly stale local copy, so a delete is undone or a fresh add is lost. Replace with per-item writes (one document or field per item) and field-level order updates; never write back a list built from stale state.
c. Delete leaves references. Deleting an item must, in ONE transaction/batch, remove the item AND every reference to it (order list/array, section membership, header/footer social-icon list, denormalized public snapshot, featured/pinned flags, and whatever feeds sitemap/JSON-LD sameAs). Dashboard and public page must read the same structure.
d. Denormalized snapshot not rebuilt. If a "published/public profile" snapshot exists, rebuild it in the same publish step (W1.3) - or remove the snapshot and read live data.
e. Filter/renderer mismatch. The public query or renderer excludes valid new items (requires a url, wrong enabled flag, type not in an allow-list, schedule/visibility rules, missing order) or includes deleted ones; a renderer switch that lacks a case for some item types silently renders nothing.
f. Ordering. Concurrent adds/reorders create duplicate or missing order values and hide or misplace items. Use a safe scheme (sparse/fractional order values, or a transaction that renumbers) so no item is ever dropped or duplicated; items with missing order values get a deterministic position.
g. Optimistic UI without confirmation. The dashboard shows the change before the server confirms and never rolls back on failure; "Saved" appears on failure; non-2xx responses are ignored; errors are swallowed in empty catch blocks.
h. Race conditions. Add followed quickly by delete (before the add's response) leaves an orphan or resurrects the item. Use client-generated stable ids (idempotent creates), serialize mutations per user or per item, and ignore out-of-order responses.
i. Dashboard data cache. Query keys not updated/invalidated after a mutation, or a refetch racing with an optimistic update brings back the old list.
j. Write-path basics. Writes not awaited before the response is returned on serverless hosting; the Admin SDK used on an edge runtime; the Clerk user id not detected on the server (middleware matcher or helper usage) so the handler returns early; undefined values or oversized documents rejected by the database; wrong project/env vars in the hosting environment. Confirm every mutation by reading the document back from Firebase.

W1.3 ONE MUTATION PATH + ONE PUBLISH STEP. All item mutations (every type, from every entry point including the "Paste a link" box and the "Add to your page" sheet) go through the same server-side functions that: (1) verify the Clerk session and that the caller owns the profile/item; (2) validate and sanitize input per item type; (3) write with transactions/batches (field-level updates, server timestamps); (4) run ONE publish step: rebuild the snapshot (if any), revalidate the public page, refresh the sitemap lastmod, and queue search-engine notification (W4.7); (5) return the saved item(s) so the client reconciles. No mutation may bypass it.

W1.4 UI TRUTHFULNESS. Optimistic updates are fine but must reconcile with the server response; on failure roll back and show the existing error toast. Use the existing loading/skeleton components for pending states.

DONE WHEN (W1): for every item type: add -> on the public page within about 5 s (hard refresh and on a second device); edit -> updated; reorder -> same order; disable -> disappears, enable -> returns; delete -> gone from Firebase (document and all references), from the dashboard and from the public page, with no reappearance after refresh, sign-out/sign-in or a later edit; rapid add/edit/delete/reorder sequences end in the correct final state; a forced failure shows an honest error and rolls back; each proven by a Firebase read-back (path + redacted fields) in the report.


